ABSTRACT Here, the interfacial synergism of discontinuous spot shaped $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous nanocomposite as electron transfer layer (ETL) underlayer is presented in highly efficient mesoscopic perovskite solar cells (M-PSCs). Based on this new strategy, strong charge recombination observed in previous $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ -based ETLs is suppressed to a great extent as the pathways of charge recombination and energy loss are blocked effectively. Meanwhile, the internal series resistance of entire M-PSC is decreased remarkably. The new ETL is more kinetically favorable to electron transfer and thus results in significant photovoltaic improvement and alleviated hysteresis effect of M-PSCs.
Keywords: $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ , interfacial synergism, electron transfer layer, perovskite, solar cell
## INTRODUCTION
Today, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are at the frontier of research in the domain of new photovoltaics, capturing more and more attention from both academic and industrial circles. As an emerging photovoltaic material, organolead halide perovskites in PSCs have demonstrated many superiorities over the traditional ones, e.g., tunable band-gap, ultralong carrier diffusion length, as well as good solution processibility that is highly desired for future cost-effective and facile fabrication [1–4]. PSCs exhibit high flexibility in cell architectures and the ones that contain mesoporous scaffolds are usually classified to be mesoscopic PSCs (M-PSCs) which are extraordinarily representative and reliable in terms of achieving high photoelectric conversion efficiencies (PCEs) [5,6]. In recent years, no efforts have been spared to promoting the device performance of M-PSCs and a stabilized PCE of $22.1\%$ has been achieved recently [7]. Nowadays, with many scientific issues being explored around M-PSCs, the pursuit of further photovoltaic improvement is imperative. Electron transfer in PSCs, including both interfacial extraction and transport, is fundamental photophysical kinetic process that is implemented for electron transfer layer (ETL) and is crucial to device performance [8–11].
The configuration of routine ETL in M-PSCs consists of two layers: (i) mesoporous scaffold that affords sufficient pore filling of perovskite and favorable electron extraction and transport; (ii) compact underlayer that plays the roles of selective transfer of electrons simultaneously effective hole blocking to minimize shunt pathway. Concerning materials, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been widely adopted in such duallayer structured ETL for M-PSCs so far [8,12–15]. Actually, many attempts have been performed in order to make ETL kinetically more favorable, e.g., surface modifications, nanostructure tailoring, and the utilizations of alternative materials with better optoelectronic properties [16–23]. Compared with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is chemically stable as well and, more importantly, its charge mobility is almost two orders of magnitude higher than that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [11,24]. On the other hand, the photocatalytic activity is less pronounced due to a higher band gap of ${\sim}3.8\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ which is favorable to the long-term stability of PSCs [25]. Herein, spontaneously the speculation whether $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based M-PSCs is really more efficient than that based on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is arising. However, even many efforts have been employed, utilizing $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as either mesoporous scaffold or compact underlayer in ETL did not make a contribution to the improvement of photovoltaic performance in M-PSCs as expected [26,27]. Thus, one important challenge we are facing is how to construct ETL for M-PSCs that will make the best use of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ .
Herein, we illustrate our systematical investigations on the optoelectronic advantages of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as $\operatorname{ETL},$ as well as the unique photovoltaic behaviors embodied in M-PSCs. Benefitting from the cooperation with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous scaffold, we report one rational strategy using $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as underlayer to reconstruct ETL for highly efficient MPSCs. The concept “interfacial synergism of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite as ETL underlayer” was put forward for the first time, which perfectly explained the facilitation of charge transfer kinetic process and the passivation of strong charge recombination. To begin with, we covered the fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) incompletely with only a spot of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites on purpose. Then, as we conducted the sequential deposition of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ colloid (for construction of mesoporous scaffold), it was inferred that a few $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were also capable to contact with FTO directly, through which an interfacial nanocomposite of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was formed and acted as a substantial ETL underlayer. Based on this new strategy, the strong charge recombination observed in previous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - based ETLs was remarkably suppressed since the pathways of charge recombination and energy loss were blocked effectively. In the meantime, the internal series resistance of entire M-PSC was decreased remarkably. Compared with traditional $\operatorname{ETL},$ this novel one was more kinetically favorable to electron transfer, thus leading to substantial photovoltaic improvement and alleviated hysteresis effect for M-PSCs. The steady-state and champion PCEs of the M-PSCs using the new ETL were $16.31\%$ and $17.58\%$ , respectively, higher than that (steadystate $\mathrm{PCE}{=}14.23\%$ ; champion $\mathrm{PCE}{=}16.37\%$ ) of M-PSCs with traditional ETLs.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Materials and reagents
Unless stated otherwise, all materials were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich or Alfa Aesar and used as received. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ paste was purchased from Dyesol (18NR-T). Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO, $15~\Omega/\mathrm{sq})$ ) was purchased from Pilkington, in USA. Spiro-MeOTAD and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ were purchased from Borun New Material Technology
Co., Ltd and Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp, respectively.
$\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ or $\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ underlayer precursor solution preparation $5.64\mathrm{g}\ S\mathrm{nCl_{2}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethyl alcohol, and the solution was stirred on a magnetic stirring apparatus for $^{3\mathrm{{h}}}$ at the temperature of $78^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ then aged for $^{3\mathrm{{h}}}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After that, the solution was put in a reagent bottle for over $^{7\mathrm{d}}$ at room temperature, and the faint yellow $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ organic sol was gotten. Then, the sol of $0.5~\mathrm{mol~L^{-1}}$ was diluted into different concentrations and used to prepare the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayers.
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ organic sol for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer was prepared according to the reported procedure [28]. Briefly, $68~\mathrm{mL}$ of tetrabutyl titanate and $16.5\mathrm{mL}$ of diethanolamine were dissolved in $210~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol, and then the mixture was stirred vigorously for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ at room temperature (Solution A). A mixture of $3.6~\mathrm{mL}$ $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and $100~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol (Solution B) was added dropwise into the Solution A under stirring. The resulting solution was aged for $24\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature, resulting in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ organic sol.
## Device fabrication
To fabricate the devices, the FTO coated glass was patterned by etching with $Z\mathrm{n}$ powder and $2~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ HCl diluted in deionized water. The etched substrate was then cleaned with $2\%$ hellmanex diluted in deionized water, rinsed with deionized water, acetone, isopropanol and deionized water and dried with clean dry air. The $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer was formed through spin coating $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ organic sol on the clean substrate at $4000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{{s},}$ followed by a sintering process in a furnace at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . A thin layer of compact anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was formed through spin coating $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ organic sol on the clean substrate at $3000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\ {\mathsf{s}},$ followed by a sintering process in a furnace at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ .
Mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was prepared by spin coating a commercial $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ paste diluted in ethanol (1:6, weight ratio) at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . After drying at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films were gradually heated to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , baked at this temperature for $30~\mathrm{min}$ and cooled to room temperature.
To prepare the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ layer, $462\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethylformamiee and dimethyl sulphoxide mixed precursor solution (before optimized 9:1 and after optimized 5:1, volume ratio) was heated at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ under magnetic stirring, and the mixture was then spin coated on the as-prepared $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film at $3000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ in glovebox. The $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ films were dropped with a solution of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ in 2-propanol $(30\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}, $ ) and spin-coated at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Afterward, the as-prepared films were heated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for around $20\mathrm{min}$ until the color changed to dark red.
The hole transport material (HTM) was then deposited by spin coating at $3000\mathrm{\rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . The HTM was prepared by dissolving $72.3~\mathrm{mg}$ spiro-MeOTAD and $28.8\upmu\mathrm{L}$ 4-tert-butylpyridine, $17.5\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of a stock solution of $520~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulphonyl)imide in acetonitrile in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ chlorobenzene. Finally, $60\ \mathrm{nm\Ag}$ was thermally evaporated on top of the device to form the back contact. The active area of this electrode was fixed at $0.105~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . All devices were fabricated in glovebox.
## Characterizations
The sample morphologies were observed using scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI QUANTA 450). UV-vis absorption spectra were measured using a spectrophotometer equipped with an integrating sphere setup (JASCOV-570). Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of ETL underlayer/FTO substrates were measured using a Keithley 2601 source. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) was performed using BRUKER Dimension Icon. $J{-}V$ curves of PSCs were measured using a Keithley 2450 source meter scanning the devices at $0.2{\mathrm{V}}{\mathrm{~s}}^{-1}$ or $0.01\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ under simulated $_{\mathrm{AM}\ 1.5\mathrm{~G~}}$ illumination $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\prime}100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , PEC-L15, Japan). $I/V$ curves of devices without perovskite were measured under dark at the scan rate of $0.2\mathrm{~V~}\ s^{-1}$ . The forward bias for stability characterization of the solar cells were maintained under maximum power point. The active area was fixed at $0.105~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ by attaching with a mask of $0.07\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted on a computer-controlled electrochemical workstation (Zennium Zahner, Germany) under white LED with light illumination of $10\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The measured frequencies ranged from $100~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $2\mathrm{MHz}$ at DC bias of $0\mathrm{v}$ and open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ . The amplitude was $10~\mathrm{mV}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Considering the strong charge recombination at $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface as aforementioned, it is reasonable to employ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ instead of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as mesoporous scaffold for M-PSCs in this work. Then our research concentrates on how to utilize $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as underlayer more rationally. Firstly, some essential investigations on morphology and optoelectronic features were carried out to deepen our knowledge of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films that were prepared on FTO glass by sol-gel route [29]. Typically, an ethanol dispersion of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol was spread on FTO glass by spin-coating, then, the film composed of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites formed after sintering at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . It was noteworthy that the control of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film thickness was realized by changing the concentrations of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol. Surface and cross-sectional morphologies of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films were characterized by SEM as demonstrated in Fig. 1. We prepared a series of samples with the precursor concentrations from 0.500 to $0.008{\ m o l\ L}^{-1}$ . It is obviously that relatively larger concentration of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol results in thicker film as observed for sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.500\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . Fig. 1a exhibits an average film thickness of about $100\mathrm{nm}$ for sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.500$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ . Despite of many pin-holes, the waved surface of FTO glass is well flattened by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites. Subsequently with precursor concentration being halved, a compact layer and surface undulations can be observed for sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.250\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1};$ , as shown in Fig. 1b. But with further decrease in precursor concentration, SEM images in Fig. 1c indicate that the surface of FTO could not be covered completely by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites in sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.125~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ . Seen from the SEM images in Fig. 1d of sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.083\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1};$ , the tendency becomes pronounced as more and more salient parts of FTO expose but the continuity of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer is maintained more or less. Sudden changes in surface morphology and film continuity appear when the precursor concentration is reduced to much lower levels, as in the cases of the following three samples shown in Fig. 1d–f respectively. For example, most of FTO surface in sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.083\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ is not covered adequately (at least apparently) and only in low-lying regions can we observe some $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites. Subsequent characterizations by AFM further support this fact. A relatively smooth surface of the bare quartz glass is illustrated in Fig. 2a, b. Then, a procedure which is consistent with that of the preparation of sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.063\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1}$ was performed by spin-coating a dilute $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol on quartz glass. Fig. 2c, d clearly show that the small-sized $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles attached onto quartz glass are dispersed in the absence of good interconnectivity, indicating incomplete coverage as
observed in SEM results.
The electrical behaviors of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ samples were evaluated by comparisons with conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer and bare FTO, through which the fact of incomplete coverage of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ on FTO surface in some samples can be further confirmed. LSV curves in Fig. 3a show the current versus applied bias voltage, which can be used to qualitatively compare and evaluate the conductive feature of film samples. Evidently, the conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact film of ca. $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness [30] has an inferior conductivity as indicated by the curve slope. In contrast, a better conductive feature of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is well reflected by the sample of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}–0.500\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ since it outputs larger current under the same bias voltage. Moreover, the decrease in thickness results in further enhancement in conductivity for sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.250$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ . Speaking of the above three samples with FTO glass completely covered, the slopes of their LSV curves change more or less along with the bias voltage especially within the range between $^{-1}$ and $1\mathrm{V}_{:}$ suggesting that the current-voltage feature shown in LSV curves arises from the intrinsic properties of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . Remarkable enhancement in conductivity is observed as the FTO surface is not completely covered by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites. For instance, a linear response of current under bias voltage is output for the sample of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.125$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ . Further with more FTO surface being exposed as in the cases of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.032\mod\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.008$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ , the two LSV curves nearly coincide with that of bare FTO. Hence, we could draw the conclusion that the sudden change as reflected in the samples of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -0.125 $\mathrm{mol~L^{-1}}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.032$ mol L–1 and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.008\mod\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ stems from the intrinsic conductive behavior of FTO. The results of LSV measurement are well consistent with SEM images and further elucidate that the FTO surfaces in the above three samples are not covered completely by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . It is verified that the properties in light transmission of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ also surpass that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Fig. 3b shows the transmittance spectra obtained by adopting the substrate glass of FTO as reference. It is clearly revealed that, compared with conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact film (sample $\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based films have shown higher transmittance from 350 to $850~\mathrm{nm}$ . In particular, the light transmittance of sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}–0.500\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (with the largest film thickness among $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based samples) even exceeds $100\%$ in a very wide range better than bare FTO, which is of great significance. This finding suggests that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is competent to perform as an anti-reflection layer on FTO, in turn allowing more photons to be collected for subsequent conversion into photocurrent [31,32]. Through the series of parallel comparisons, it can be inferred that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as ETL material possesses both electrical and optical advantages.
In principle, the underlayer of ETL in M-PSCs ought to be compact and pin-hole free so as to retard charge recombination and play the role of hole-blocking effectively [33]. If so, some samples with FTO being covered incompletely by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in this work are supposed to be undesirable. For this reason, our subsequent results are contradictory to the “universal” principle. However, sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}–0.500\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ realized full coverage of FTO by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer while Table 1 shows that the relevant M-PSCs delivered unsatisfied average PCE of $9.97\%$ as a result of lower $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ (0.81 V) and fill factor (FF) (0.55), respectively. With no doubt, strong charge recombination derived from the trap states of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ remains responsible to this issue, as reported in the literatures from our team and others. Nevertheless, when the decrease in thickness of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer leads to exposure of FTO, the promotion in photovoltaic performance is achieved.
Compared with the M-PSC based on sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.50$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ , the average $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of that based on sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.125\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ increase remarkably to $0.96\mathrm{~V~}$ and 0.66, respectively, achieving an average PCE of $13.99\%$ as a consequence. Data listed in Table 1 distinctly indicate that the photovoltaic improvement of M-PSCs in this work is ascribed to the increases in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF, which are sensitive to charge recombination and cell internal resistance. We further diluted the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol gradually and the FTO surface was exposed to a greater degree, while the average PCEs of M-PSCs rise all the same until an optimized value of $15.13\%$ is attained based on sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.032\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1}$ . Although a spot of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites are attached to FTO surface in the cases of samples $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.016\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.008\mathrm{~mol~L^{-1}};$ only slight decrease is observed in photovoltaic performance. Our results demonstrate the peculiar behaviors of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as underlayer in ETL for M-PSCs, which is quite different from that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Evidently, using $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film of ${\sim}100~\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness as underlayer is not that appropriate, because the average PCE of M-PSCs is even lower than that in the absence of any underlayer. It can be certified from Table 1 that M-PSCs containing only mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffold in ETL deliver an average PCE of $13.07\%$ , much higher than that without $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact underlayer as reported previously [34]. Above findings interest us and we decide to go deep into the researches on the underlayer in ETL, by which further developments of device performance in MPSCs are expected.
A practice we widely adopt during $J{-}V$ (photocurrentvoltage) measurement is to mask the active area of one solar cell. Concerning M-PSCs with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as underlayer in $\operatorname{ETL},$ another unique phenomenon we can observe is the pseudo photocurrent and PCE when the active area is unmasked, which is more striking especially in the case of thicker $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films. Fig. 4 shows the $J{-}V$ curves of M-PSCs with active area masked or un-masked. Detailed photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 2. Device based on sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.500\ \mathrm{\mol\L^{-1}}$ gives a pseudo photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $41.87~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (a pseudo
PCE of $14.67\%$ ), much larger than the veritable value of $23.97\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Despite alleviated by the decrease in the thickness of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer, the pseudo $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE are still notable as exhibited in the case of sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.250$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ that covers FTO completely. However, the pseudo $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE become negligible when sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.063$ $\mathrm{\mol{L}^{-1}}$ is applied, which cannot realize the full coverage of FTO. Likewise for M-PSCs using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ underlayer, it has been reported that the critical parameters would not be affected whether a mask was used in $J{-}V$ measurement or not. Actually, it is the highly conductive carrier transport materials such as $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in this work that should be responsible to the pseudo photocurrent as observed in bulk heterojunction and other hybrid solar cells. Hence, the high conductivity of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ together with better interparticle connectivity facilitates the formation of 2D transport channels. If no mask is applied in $J/\negmedspace V$ measurement, the 2D transport channels enable the collection of photogenerated electrons from adjacent “inactive areas” under illumination. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as underlayer is unlikely to be able to form 2D transport channels available because of its poor conductivity, and in the meantime, it is also unachievable for some $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ samples in this work (such as $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.063\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1})$ ) due to the discontinuities. Therefore, it is acceptable that pseudo photocurrent appears in MPSCs based on samples $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}–0.500\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - $0.250\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , in which the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF are also affected under different measuring conditions as shown in Table 2. As active sites for charge recombination, the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface states can be passivated by photogenerated electrons, and then, it is very clear why higher $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF can be obtained for M-PSCs if measured with all area illuminated.
The architecture of M-PSCs is shown in Scheme 1, in which the ETL is extracted for a close-up illustration. Despite the incomplete covering of FTO by $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites, we never deny the significance of full coverage of FTO in terms of hole-blocking and electron transfer. Therefore, a new perspective is put forward: interfacial synergism of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite. Taking the sample of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.032\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ as example, the largely diluted $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol is spreaded onto FTO but fails to fully cover the substrate. After the sequential spin-coating of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sol (to prepare mesoporous scaffold), some of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites are inferred to fill the interspace where FTO is exposed. Under this synergism, an interfacial layer of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite is achieved (see the top-view image in Scheme 1) which is tightly attached to FTO surface acting as effective underlayer in ETL for M-PSCs actually.
Herein, two types of devices are designed to evaluate the capability of hole-blocking, as depicted by Fig. 5a, c, respectively. The difference between the two architectures is the incorporation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous scaffold in the second one. As observed in Fig. 5b, d, these two types of devices both generate larger dark current in the absence of any underlayer, suggesting that this process is governed by the direct contact of Spiro-MeOTAD with FTO. In terms of the first device, the dark current can be effectively reduced even with a spot of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites attached to FTO for the samples like $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.016\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.032\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1}$ . However, full coverage of FTO by sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}–0.250\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ does not bring about further improvement, and the decrease of dark current is not as much as another two samples $(\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.125~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.063\mathrm{~mol~L^{-1}}\rangle$ instead. What we can deduce from the findings above is that the surface trap states of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ probably provide another route for dark current in addition to direct contact. The second type of testing device is more equivalent to the real architecture of M-PSCs and the regularity demonstrated in Fig. 5d is distinct from that based on the first one. It can be acknowledged that the dark current decreases regularly in the order of dilutions of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sol concentrations, from sample $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.250$ $\mathrm{mol~L^{-1}}$ to $\mathrm{SnO}_{2^{-}}0.032\mathrm{~mol~L}^{-1}$ . This evidence powerfully supports our viewpoint on the interfacial synergism based on which the incontinuous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer together with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous scaffold can cooperate well to effectively play the role of hole-blocking.
reproductivity is obtained.
EIS characterization was used to elucidate the charge transfer dynamics within M-PSCs [36,37]. Nyquist plots in Fig. 6 were measured under light illumination of $10\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ at DC bias of $0\mathrm{v}$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}},$ respectively. The fitted equivalent circuit model is composed of series resistance $(R_{s})$ , two components for transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{trans}})$ at the ETL/perovskite and the perovskite/HTL interfaces, and recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ forming a parallel circuit with capacitors. The $R_{\mathrm{trans}}$ is ascribed to high-frequency arc and the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is assigned to low-frequency arc [31,38,39]. Since the only difference among the three devices lies in the microstructure between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous scaffold and FTO, we believe the difference of impedance spectra is in direct relevance to different charge transfer behaviors at the interface. In the case of short circuit (under a bias of $0\mathrm{V}$ ), M-PSCs based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ show a much higher $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ $(12,613\Omega)$ than that of the freebased device $\left(3301\Omega\right)$ , indicating that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ interfacial nanocomposite ETL underlayer could effectively block electrons transfer from FTO to perovskite and in turn minimize energy loss. In addition, the $R_{\mathrm{trans}}$ and $R_{s}$ of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based device, either at the bias of $0\mathrm{v}$ or $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ (Fig. $6\mathsf{a},\mathsf{b}\mathsf{\lambda}$ ), are similar with the one without underlayer, but lower than the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ devices, implying that the photogenerated electrons can transfer from perovskite to FTO more conveniently when using our $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based ETL architecture. Thus, it can be concluded that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based ETL could decrease electron transfer resistance and meanwhile effectively suppress charge recombination, which endows M-PSCs with more superior photovoltaic performance.
Fig. 7a, b illustrates the $J{-}V$ curves and detailed parameters of the champion cells from each group, as well as their steady-state PCE output under maximum power point. It can be found that, based on reverse scan, a considerable PCE of $15.77\%$ is achieved by the champion cell of the underlayer-free group, only slightly lower than that $(16.37\%)$ of the one using conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ underlayer. Whereas, the two champion cells behave differently in their steady-state outputs ( $10.78\%$ versus $14.23\%$ ) under an identical applied voltage of $0.85\mathrm{V}$ as shown in Fig. 7b. This experimental fact convincingly supports the necessity of underlayer for efficient electron collection in M-PSCs of working status. As we can see, PSC with the best photovoltaic performance is the one based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer that endows M-PSCs with the highest PCE of $17.58\%$ in this work. High values of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ $\left(1.08{\mathrm{V}}\right)$ and FF (0.78) both benefit from the major roles that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer plays in hole-blocking and reducing internal series resistance, due to which one steady-state PCE of $16.31\%$ is output under an applied voltage of $0.90{\mathrm{V}}$ . We then tested the long term stability of three kinds M-PSCs based on different ETLs. These devices were stored at ambient air of high relative humidity $\mathrm{(rH)}$ (at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $60\%$ rH) in the dark, and the photovoltaic parameters were tracked each day. From Fig. 7c, we can find that the M-PSCs based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer shows a better stability than the other two devices. After $6\mathrm{d}.$ , the M-PSCs on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer and the one free of underlayer demonstrated a notable decrease to ca. $25\%$ of their initial PCEs. By comparison, the performance of the one using $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer shows no degradation. This might be attributed to the unfavorable electron extraction property of ETL with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer or without compact layer, which leave many electrons accumulated near the ETL to decompose the perovskite [40]. On the contrary, the fast extraction of electrons through the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ synergistic ETL would hardly accumulate electrons at the interface near underlayer and thus construct a more stable device. In addition, it has been reported previously that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is easy to be excited by UV light to accelerate the decomposition of perovskite [41]. As aforementioned, the photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is less pronounced due to a higher band gap of ${\sim}3.8\mathrm{eV}$ , which is favorable to the device’s stability when exposed to UV light.
In planar or mesoporous PSCs, hysteresis effect in current-voltage characterizations is a common phenomenon observed under different conditions including scan directions and rates, as well as applying bias voltage and light soaking prior to measurement [42,43]. To date, many reasons have been indicated to be the causes of hysteresis behavior, such as ion migration and transient ferroelectric polarization [44]. On the other hand, any factor disadvantageous to charge transfer kinetic processes can also result in pronounced hysteresis, like serious charge recombination, slow transport, and less efficient interfacial charge extraction [45,46]. Herein we focus specifically on revealing the influence of ETL underlayer on the hysteresis behavior. Fig. 8 illustrates the J$V$ curves of M-PSCs under two different scan rates, and detailed parameters are summarized in Table 4. In the absence of underlayer, it is confirmed that the hysteresis behavior of M-PSCs is very obvious where the gap between the parameters obtained under is sizeable. Here, we adopt a modified hysteresis index (HI) defined by Equation (1) as follows to demonstrate the hysteresis effect quantitatively:
$$
\mathrm{HI}=\frac{J_{\mathrm{RS}}(0.8V_{\mathrm{OC}})-J_{\mathrm{FS}}(0.8V_{\mathrm{OC}})}{J_{\mathrm{RS}}(0.8V_{\mathrm{OC}})},
$$
where $J_{\mathrm{RS}}$ $(0.8V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ and $J_{\mathrm{FS}}$ $(0.8V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ stand for the photocurrent density at $80\%$ of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ for the reverse scan and forward scan, respectively. At a faster scan rate of $0.20\mathrm{V}\ s^{-1}$ , the $V_{\mathrm{OC}},$ FF and PCE taken from the forward $J.$ $V$ curve are only $0.84\mathrm{V}$ , 0.39 and $6.82\%$ , respectively, much lower than that ( $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{OC}=1.10V}$ , $\mathrm{FF}=0.75$ , $\mathrm{PCE}=$ $15.56\%$ ) under reverse scan at the same rate yielding a very high HI of 0.475 consequently. At a slower scan rate of $0.01\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ , HI is reduced remarkably to 0.202, in accordance with that reported previously. By comparison, the hysteresis effect is suppressed a lot for the M-PSCs with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ underlayer, with relevant HIs decreased to 0.220 and 0.165 at the two scan rates of 0.20 and $0.01\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ . The improvement obviously stems from the significant effect of underlayer on electron transfer dynamics, in particular on the retardation of charge recombination through hole-blocking. As mentioned above, the synergism of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ makes the underlayer more favorable to electron transfer. Thus, it is easy to understand why $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ based M-PSCs present the slightest hysteresis behavior, with a very small HI of 0.082 at the scan rate of $0.01\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ shown in Fig. 8f.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, in this work we demonstrated comprehensive investigations on the optoelectronic properties of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . Moreover, one rational strategy for using $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ combined with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous scaffold as ETL underlayer in M-PSCs was presented. Very different from traditional ETLs in which we always necessitated one compact underlayer, it was found that only a spot of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites attached to FTO, pitifully inadequate to cover FTO completely, were sufficient to play the roles of hole-blocking and electron transfer. Subsequently, one mechanism was put forward to explain this finding: the interfacial synergism of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite as underlayer, where $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles came from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoporous scaffold and contacted with FTO directly. Based on this new strategy, strong charge recombination observed in previous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based ETLs is suppressed remarkably, as the pathways of charge recombination and energy loss were blocked effectively, and meanwhile, the internal series resistance of entire M-PSCs was minimized as much as possible. Compared with traditional ETL, the new one was more kinetically favorable to electron transfer and thus resulted in substantial photovoltaic improvement and alleviated hysteresis effect for M-PSCs. Finally, the steady-state and champion PCEs of the MPSCs using the new ETL were $16.31\%$ and $17.58\%$ , respectively, higher than that (steady-state $\mathrm{PCE}{=}14.23\%$ ; champion $\mathrm{PCE}{=}16.37\%$ ) of M-PSCs with traditional ETL. We believe that our strategy to re-construct ETLs is meaningful and it paves a new way for further developments in device performance.
